and the reason dictionary time complexity&nbsp;
equations are so funky has to do with one&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:25.280 --> 02:11:31.440
small problem. What happens when we run two&nbsp;
different dictionary keys into a hash function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:31.440 --> 02:11:38.000
and the computer tells us to store them at the&nbsp;
same index location. For example, let's say we&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:38.000 --> 02:11:43.920
have two dictionary keys with the strings, Steven&nbsp;
and Shawn. And when we run both of them through&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:43.920 --> 02:11:49.040
our hash function, the computer instructs us to&nbsp;
store both of them at the ninth index location,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:49.760 --> 02:11:55.200
this should be impossible. How are we supposed&nbsp;
to put both of them at index location nine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:56.000 --> 02:12:01.200
This little problem is what's known as a hash&nbsp;
collision and can be solved one of two ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:01.200 --> 02:12:08.000
either open addressing or closed addressing. With&nbsp;
open addressing, we just put the key in some other&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:08.000 --> 02:12:14.160
index location separate from the one returned to&nbsp;
us by the hash function. This is usually done by&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:14.160 --> 02:12:20.400
looking for the next nil value in the table,&nbsp;
ie the closest location which contains no key.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:21.440 --> 02:12:27.520
So with our example, the key Steven would&nbsp;
get hash to the index location nine, mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:27.520 --> 02:12:33.360
because it's a better name. And the inferior&nbsp;
key Shawn would get stored at index location 10,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:33.360 --> 02:12:39.840
because it's the closest open location available.&nbsp;
This does make it harder to interact with the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:39.840 --> 02:12:44.320
in our dictionary later on, and can lead to&nbsp;
problems if we eventually have a value which&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:44.320 --> 02:12:50.320
hashes to the index location 10, which is why&nbsp;
computer scientists developed closed addressing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:52.160 --> 02:12:57.520
Closed addressing uses linked lists to chain&nbsp;
together keys that result in the same hash value.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:58.320 --> 02:13:04.480
So the keys Steven and Shawn would get stored&nbsp;
together in a linked list and index location nine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:05.360 --> 02:13:09.840
The main drawback to this is that whenever we&nbsp;
want to interact with the value stored within a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:09.840 --> 02:13:15.520
key value pair for either Steven or Shawn, we end&nbsp;
up having to look through the linked list for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

