it to the 76 node and see that it's greater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:55.920 --> 02:44:02.000
so it gets swapped. And then we compare it&nbsp;
again, this time to the 90 node. And since&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:02.000 --> 02:44:06.399
it's also greater than that integer, it gets&nbsp;
swapped yet again to become the root node,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:06.960 --> 02:44:13.439
signifying it is the greatest node in the heap.&nbsp;
As you can see, it's a pretty simple concept, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:13.439 --> 02:44:18.559
add a node and then keep trying to swap up until&nbsp;
the node you've added is in its rightful place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:20.479 --> 02:44:24.240
Deleting from heap is also pretty&nbsp;
simple, at least in our case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:24.960 --> 02:44:29.600
This is because the type of deletion I want&nbsp;
to talk about is just the process of removing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:29.600 --> 02:44:35.840
the root node from the heap. And you'll see why&nbsp;
later on. To delete the root node from a heap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:35.840 --> 02:44:41.440
you follow a three step process. Step one is&nbsp;
actually removing the root node from our heap.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:42.000 --> 02:44:47.920
So in our case, we delete the 100 node. What&nbsp;
you do with it is up to the programmer, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:47.920 --> 02:44:55.200
can return it back to the user stored somewhere&nbsp;
else, print it out, etc, etc. Either way, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:55.200 --> 02:45:01.679
step two is replacing it with a node furthest to&nbsp;
the right in this case It would be the 76th note.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:03.040 --> 02:45:09.279
Finally, for step three, we do what's known as&nbsp;
a heapify. To fix up the heap, we start with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:09.279 --> 02:45:15.840
root node and compare it to its children to see&nbsp;
if we need to swap any values. So for the 76 node,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:15.840 --> 02:45:22.000
we compare it to its two child node. And since&nbsp;
76 is less than 90, we end up swapping those two.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:23.279 --> 02:45:29.519
Then we wash rinse, repeat for every subtree&nbsp;
that we have. So on the right side, we swapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:29.520 --> 02:45:36.000
90 with 76. But since 76, remains the greatest&nbsp;
integer on that particular subtree, it stays in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:36.000 --> 02:45:42.880
the same spot. On the left side, we didn't change&nbsp;
anything, but 45 is still the greatest integer&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:42.880 --> 02:45:48.160
amongst the three nodes in that subtree. So no&nbsp;
swapping is necessary on that branch of the heap.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:49.359 --> 02:45:53.839
And so we've completed our heapify, and&nbsp;
all heap properties are still intact.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:54.800 --> 02:45:58.960
That is inserting and deleting nodes in&nbsp;
a nutshell. Now let's talk about how we&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:58.960 --> 02:46:04.960
can use this to our advantage. Heaps are most&nbsp;
commonly used in the implementation of heapsort.&nbsp;&nbsp;

