heapsort is a sorting algorithm which takes&nbsp;
in a list of elements, builds them into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:10.640 --> 02:46:15.440
min or max heap, and then removes the root&nbsp;
node continuously to make a sorted list.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:16.319 --> 02:46:20.639
Because heaps always start with the minimum&nbsp;
or maximum value contained within them at&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:20.640 --> 02:46:25.520
the root node, we're able to simply just&nbsp;
remove the root node over and over again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:26.080 --> 02:46:31.279
keep refining the data structure after every pass.&nbsp;
And after every element has been removed, we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:31.279 --> 02:46:37.279
left with a sorted list. On your screen, you'll&nbsp;
see we have an unsorted list on the left. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:37.279 --> 02:46:42.880
middle, we've inserted all of those integers, and&nbsp;
in doing so created a max heap. And then finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:42.880 --> 02:46:48.160
on the right, we have continuously removed those&nbsp;
elements from the root node into a now sorted&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:48.160 --> 02:46:53.279
list. heapsort is a really cool algorithm,&nbsp;
and it will be part of our upcoming series&nbsp;&nbsp;

